{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778197644,"nonce":"fVrUVfNYsST/WCtwqjLQtYA02KU=","message":"**`MechanicsState` is the prototype of this pattern.** Surfaced from a recent conversation about why `Mechanics` keeps a structured `MechanicsState(plant, effector, solver)` rather than decomposing its sub-pieces into separate `Component`s with individual `StateIndex` cells.\n\nThe clarifying observation: `plant`/`effector`/`solver` *are* compositional, but in an equation-/ODE-structured way — kinematics update → ODE solver → effector extraction is a tightly-coupled equation pipeline, not a graph of independently-schedulable Components. The right generalisation is exactly the DAE/acausal subgraph concept this issue describes: internally compositional via equation-graph semantics; externally a single causal Component with a single structured state.\n\nSo `MechanicsState` is the de-facto answer for one specific equation-structured subgraph (musculoskeletal-like). When this issue lands, the pattern becomes general: each DAE/acausal subgraph type would expose its outer-graph state as a single keyed cell holding a domain-specific structured `Module` (à la `MechanicsState`), with internal acausal composition hidden behind it.\n\n**Implication for state-design.** This issue's design should make the relationship explicit — that the outer-graph `StateIndex` granularity stays per-subgraph, and the structured-state-per-subgraph pattern (current `MechanicsState`/`ChannelState`) extends naturally rather than being replaced. Useful prior art: `Channel` and `DelayLine` already follow the same one-StateIndex-per-Component pattern with structured internal substate.\n\nCross-ref: see also `f385489` (`AbstractState` typing) for the typing side of the same question.","files":null}]}